<b>Interview 87: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;：Animal rights volunteer, Male, born in the 1980’s, from Tianjin (Monologue)</b>

<p>It is not entirely due to my wife &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; that I converted to Islam. I was exposed to Islam much earlier, but at the time I did not consider the possibility of the conversion. My understanding of it, however, grew more profound since I met her. Before the college entrance exam, in my final year of high-school, we eloped together away from home. We felt tired of the same old life. We thought we could go very far, but because we did not have enough money, we ended up in Shijiazhuang, for less than 4 months. We ran away from home under the pressure from the exam but moreover out of family reasons, particularly her mother’s disapproval of our relationship. We did not take the college entrance exam until the next spring.   

<p>The four months while we were away, three month was spent in Shijiazhuang and a bit more than ten days in Taiyuan. When we left, we only took 300 RMB. Only one of our classmates knew about it. He managed to borrow about 1000 RMB to help us. When he saw us off at the train station, I told him I did not want to take the money, but he insisted and stealthily put it somewhere with me. Neither of us wrote to or called our families. After we ran away, our classmates were all aware of it. The situation in Tianjin was absolutely mad. I was not the only child of my family. Neither was she. Her family’s reaction was more resilient than mine. My mother could not let me go. She is 53 years old now, but she cannot bear it if someone mentions it.  

<p>We rented a house in Shijiazhuang through a housing agent. We found a place far from the center of the city, 230 RMB per month. The payment was due every month. According to the local rules, the agent took 80 RMB from us. We started to look for jobs. I found a job, but it gave up within a few days. In the beginning though, when we did not consider doing a job for long, I ran some errands for the advertising agency which does ads on massive billboards. I never made it. My basic wage was very low. It was 300 RMB to begin with, but I only got a little over 200 RMB in the end. We bought a second-hand bicycle. I would look at the map and my girlfriend sat on the back seat. We did not spend much on food. We did not feel any despair. We never thought what if we could not even afford to eat tomorrow.   

<p>Bored of Tianjin, where we could not make it, we went to Taiyuan. Also, we called a classmate of ours on his birthday. He saw the regional code of the number and knew where we were, so we had to leave. The average wage in Taiyuan is even lower than in Shijiazhuang. We could not find any jobs, therefore, we could not afford to eat. There was no way we could stay there. We called home. When we called home before, my mother could not take the phone call but cried. My father did not want to force me to go back, he only told me to be safe. My mother retired because of her illness. She was not very well. My father could still work, he retired but was hired again. Our house line did not have the function of caller ID display. My parents specifically required this function from the phone company, so that they could find out whereabouts we were. They came to look for us. My wife’s mother and my father together took us home.

<p>The times in Taiyuan were beyond mention. It was far too bitter. Also, since we spent far too much time together, and we started to argue. Before Taiyuan, we hardly ever argued. We did not share the same views on some matters, therefore there was more tension between us. Now we fight even more intensely, verbally. But it does not take long for us to make up. 

<p>I did not believe in Islam until I went back to Tian Jin. My wife’s mother could not accept me in the beginning. She was much pious. When she realized that we could not grow apart no matter what, she made an exception of the rules and let me in their house. She introduced Islam to me, from the very basics. I was intrigued.  

<p>I have always been fond of cats, among other animals. Such fondness was nothing special, nor was it very intense. My wife and I started to live together when we were in the second or the third year of university. We adopted some wild cats. We have been together since the second year in high school. We joked that we got married before we fell in love, because we started living together very early. A few months after we graduated, we got married for real. I have tried to be a veterinarian, and do some business. She was not a professional either. But as a girl, it was easier for her to find a temporary job than I. She made more money than me.  

<p>Last March, the society for cat lovers was founded. We could not afford to deal with so many sick cats. We wanted to find an organization for help. But as we looked for it, we found that there was nothing. So we might as well just found an organization by ourselves. To our surprise that many a people reacted rapidly. So now we work as veterinarians in an animal hospital. Before when we were running the society, we lead an irregular lifestyle, extremely busy at times, extremely vacant at times. But now since we worked in the hospital, we have had a fixed schedule and lived regular hours again.  In the first month, the hospital was quite alright. Everything is difficult before it becomes easy. We much relied on the friends we made through running the society for cat lovers. We only operated at home in the beginning, but now we do operations in the 120-square meter-hospital, under the administration of the Animal Husbandry and Fishery Bureau, which issued the license for our clinic. Most vets love animals, but the industry needs more regularity. In Tianjin, there are many unlawful vets. Some are dog dealers, some are not entirely capable of operating on animals, but do so purely out of monetary interests, completely devoid of morals as doctors.  

<p>I have not thought about the future too much. It is a pleasure to come here. It is easier than I imagined to get a visa. I do not know much about the exhibition though.  

<p>So few people are here, especially at night. It looks lively, but when I walk over to have a look, there were not so many people at all. Unlike in Tianjin.

